# Globals libc exports that no libc MEMBER references, so the archive's
# dead-strip would drop them, but something outside the archive does.
#
# fgx_ar's `index` decides a data global is live if another MEMBER imports it.
# A reference from a ROOT -- the tool's own .s, or libfgx.s, both of which the
# selector is given with --root -- does not count, because the index is built
# before any root is known.  --public is the declared answer for that case:
# "globals no member references but a tool might" (fgx_ar.py, the help text of
# `index --public').
#
# sys_siglist -- 2.11BSD's signal-name table (libc_c/siglist.c).  A DATA-ONLY
# member: it has no function to FGX2_EXPORT, and nothing inside libc reads it,
# so the index has neither of the two reasons it keeps a global.  Every reader
# is a TOOL -- mount(8) reports a mount_ufs child that died on a signal --
# which is exactly the case this file's header describes.
sys_siglist
#
# _ctype_ -- <ctype.h>'s class table (libc_c/ctype_.c).  A DATA-ONLY member
# like sys_siglist, read by the classifier macros in whatever includes
# <ctype.h>, which is mostly tools.
_ctype_
#
# __lc_fltused -- the name fcc's `]__lc_fltused' marker gives a program that
# generated floating point code (libc_c/fltpr.c).  No member references it; a
# PROGRAM does, and that reference is what selects fltpr, as `fltused' selects
# fltpr.s in 2.11BSD.
__lc_fltused
